{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_3ryhtskvt3xmkonphku3soyzjq7mdycvavz4drll7iyq6r23bvya","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Build and test both succeed. Here's a summary:\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the C ucontext-based stackful coroutine library in safe (mostly) Rust by replacing the platform-specific stack-swapping with **OS threads + `Mutex`/`Condvar` synchronization** so only one of {main, current coroutine} runs at a time — preserving the cooperative, non-concurrent semantics of the original.\n\n### Key design points\n- **`coroutine_open`** allocates the schedule with a 1 MiB stack buffer and `DEFAULT_COROUTINE` (16) initial slots, mirroring the C struct layout.\n- **`coroutine_new`** matches the C grow-by-doubling and `(i + nco) % cap` slot search.\n- **`coroutine_resume`** on `READY`: spawns a thread that waits to be activated, then runs the user function via a raw pointer to the schedule (safe because main is parked while the coroutine runs). On `SUSPEND`: signals the existing thread to resume.\n- **`coroutine_yield`** flips status to `SUSPEND`, sets `running = -1`, and waits to be re-activated — the equivalent of `swapcontext` back to main.\n- After the user function returns, the coroutine thread clears its own slot, decrements `nco`, sets `running = -1`, and signals main — exactly matching `mainfunc` in the C version.\n- A small global registry (`OnceLock<Mutex<HashMap<(addr,id), CoState>>>`) lets `yield` and the `SUSPEND` branch of `resume` find the right `Condvar`.\n- `coroutine_close` removes registry entries belonging to the schedule and drops it.\n\n### Use of `unsafe`\nLimited to the irreducible bits: passing the schedule pointer across the thread boundary as a `usize` (via a `Send` wrapper) and dereferencing it on the coroutine thread. This is sound because the synchronization primitives guarantee the pointer is only dereferenced by one thread at a time.\n\n### Verification\n